[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A node whose inputs changed should go dirty rather than quietly stale

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MECH-DIRTY-EDGE-ON-OPTION-CHANGE
TITLE: A node whose inputs changed should go dirty rather than quietly stale
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The operator's, and it belongs with the behaviour model rather than the grammar
surface. Once file relations are settled, a change to what a node depends on
should mark that node DIRTY -- visibly needing reconciliation -- rather than
leaving it silently stale, with a model filling the gap afterwards.

The fingerprint machinery already does this for node-to-node contracts. What it
does not cover is a relation pointing at a file, which today resolves to a path
that nothing checks for existence, let alone for change. Scope this where the
behaviour model lands.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the point of the dirty-edge idea in the operator's semantics rather
than an incidental feature: staleness that announces itself is the difference
between a graph that decays and one that asks to be repaired.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Retyped from MECHANISM to REQUIREMENT on 2026-08-30 by the mechanism mood test under DEC-NODE-FAMILIES, with medium confidence after a second opinion: it can be violated: a counterexample would be fixed in the world, not in this node. Noted for a later split: 'The fingerprint machinery already does this for node-to-node contracts. What it does not cover is a relation pointing at a file, which today resolves to a path that nothing checks for existence, let alone for change.' -- a description of the existing mechanism and its gap; 'Scope this where the behaviour model lands' is WORK. The UID keeps its prefix: DEC-UID-OUTLIVES-TYPE.
